userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add turn blocking, attachment-preserving prompt context and per-entry deadlines to the native loop.

**Motivation** — The only way to influence a native-agent tool call today is an in-process JavaScript plugin providing `loop-handlers`.

**Approach** — Two small `packages/nax-agent` additions make this possible:

1. `BeforeTurnPatch` gains `block` and `promptContext`. A `block` patch makes `runNativeTurn` throw before it appends anything to the transcript or makes a provider request; chat already renders a turn that throws as an error line `CODE: message` (`applyTurnEnd` in `packages/nax/src/tui/chat/transcript-model.ts`), and a run treats it as a failed turn. `promptContext` is prefixed to the prompt message: the existing `seed` patch cannot be used for context because it REPLACES the prompt message and drops its images (`beforeTurnSeed` in `turn-loop.ts`).
2. `LoopHandlerEntry` gains an optional `timeoutMs` that `wrapExternalHandler` uses instead of `LOOP_HANDLER_TIMEOUT_MS` (10 s). A formatter or type check after an edit routinely needs longer; plugin handlers that do not set it keep 10 s.

`runNativeTurn` (`native/session/turn-loop.ts`) — after the `before_turn` dispatch: a patch with `block` throws `NaxError` code `NATIVE_TURN_BLOCKED` (message = the reason, context `{ stage: "native-session", sessionName }`) before `messages.push(...)`; a patch with a non-empty `promptContext` prefixes `promptContext + "\n\n"` to the content of the first message `beforeTurnSeed` returns.

`raceSettlement` in `wrapExternalHandler` (`loop-events/external-handler.ts`) — deadline = `entry.timeoutMs` when it is a finite positive number, else `_externalHandlerDeps.timeoutMs`.

**Complexity.** `runNativeTurn` is not in the nax-agent complexity baseline, so the `block` check and the `promptContext` prefix live in helpers (for example by extending `beforeTurnSeed`), not as new branches in `runNativeTurn`.

The factory handler itself does not settle within its `timeoutMs` (unreachable while every hook honours its own deadline): existing wrapper behaviour: `before_tool` fails closed to block, other events change nothing. Preserve abort/timer cleanup and all existing handler defaults.

**Scope** — In: the four existing loop files, their behavioral tests and any required existing API declaration snapshot update. Out: hook config and execution (US-002/003), factory producers (US-004/005), chat/run wiring (US-006/007); 
- Concatenating `before_turn` `seed` or `promptContext` patches across several handlers; `dispatchChain` keeps overwrite semantics, so a later plugin handler returning `promptContext` replaces the agent hooks' value.

**Interface** — Target additions to the existing types, retaining all existing members:
```typescript
export interface BeforeTurnPatch {
  readonly seed?: readonly NativeTranscriptMessage[];
  readonly history?: readonly NativeTranscriptMessage[];
  readonly block?: { readonly reason: string };
  readonly promptContext?: string;
}
export interface LoopHandlerEntry {
  readonly plugin: string;
  readonly event: LoopEvent;
  readonly handler: ExternalHandlerOf<LoopEvent>;
  readonly timeoutMs?: number;
}
```
`PATCHABLE_FIELDS.before_turn` target: `["seed", "history", "block", "promptContext"]`.

**Goal** — Introduce the agent hook contracts and a tolerant zod-backed loader for the new hooks.json section.

**Motivation** — Claude Code users expect a shell command in config.

**Approach** — `hooks.json` (global `~/.nax/hooks.json` via `globalConfigDir()`, project `<repoRoot>/.nax/hooks.json`). The lifecycle `hooks` map is unchanged; the new `agent` key holds arrays of entries per event.

Entry fields:

| Field | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `command` | non-empty string | required | command line, argv-spawned |
| `matcher` | string | absent = every tool | `"*"` means every tool (checked before any compile); any other value is a regular expression that must match the WHOLE tool name: at load both the raw value and its wrapped form `^(?:matcher)$` must compile, else the entry is skipped. Read for `PreToolUse` and `PostToolUse` only |
| `scope` | non-empty array of `"chat"` / `"run"` | `["chat", "run"]` | surfaces the entry runs on |
| `timeout` | integer ms, 1..600000 | 30000 | per-hook deadline |
| `enabled` | boolean | true | `false` drops the entry at load |

`skipGlobal: true` in the project file skips the global file's `agent` section exactly as it already skips its lifecycle `hooks`. Global entries run before project entries.

`loadAgentHooksConfig` reads each file with `Bun.file(...).text()` and `JSON.parse` inside its own try/catch, not `loadJsonFile` (which would log a second warning under the `hooks` stage).

Missing hooks.json or no agent key contributes no hooks and no warning. Invalid JSON, non-object top-level values, or non-object agent sections each contribute no hooks from that file and one agent-hooks warning naming its path. Unknown event keys are ignored with one warning naming the key and path. An entry fails validation (missing command, bad scope, timeout out of range, matcher not a valid regular expression): entry skipped; one warning naming the event, index and origin; other entries still load. Treat arrays and null as non-objects for config/object contracts. Return an empty array for every event even when neither file contributes hooks.

**Constants by ruling.** The entry default `30000` ms and the three `AGENT_HOOK_*` constants are constants by ruling, not `NaxConfig` keys, for the same reason `LOOP_HANDLER_TIMEOUT_MS` is: they bound liveness rather than being per-project policy; a user tunes a hook through its own `timeout`.

Barrel: each story adds its new module's exports to `packages/nax/src/hooks/agent/index.ts` (created by US-002), and `packages/nax/src/hooks/index.ts` re-exports them; `cli/chat.ts` and `run-rules-setup.ts` import from `@/hooks`, never from an internal file.

**Scope** — In: types.ts, config.ts, the new agent barrel, re-export from the existing hooks barrel and config behavior tests. Out: process execution/trust (US-003), handlers (US-004/005), loader facade/chat wiring (US-006), run wiring (US-007); no changes to lifecycle hook schemas.
- Any change to the run lifecycle hooks (`on-*` events), `loadHooksConfig`, `executeHook` or `fireHook`.

**Interface** —
```typescript
// types.ts
export const AGENT_HOOK_EVENTS = ["PreToolUse", "PostToolUse", "UserPromptSubmit", "SessionStart", "Stop"] as const;
export type AgentHookEvent = (typeof AGENT_HOOK_EVENTS)[number];
export type AgentHookSurface = "chat" | "run";
export interface AgentHookEntry {
  readonly command: string;
  readonly matcher?: string;
  readonly scope: readonly AgentHookSurface[];
  readonly timeoutMs: number;
  readonly origin: "global" | "project";
}
export type AgentHooksConfig = { readonly [E in AgentHookEvent]: readonly AgentHookEntry[] };
export type AgentHookRun =
  | { readonly kind: "ok"; readonly stdout: string; readonly json?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>> }
  | { readonly kind: "block"; readonly reason: string }
  | { readonly kind: "error"; readonly reason: string };

// config.ts
export function loadAgentHooksConfig(projectNaxDir: string, globalDir?: string): Promise<AgentHooksConfig>;
```

**Goal** — Execute one configured hook and return a normalized result with trust enforcement, bounded process cleanup and attributed logging.

**Motivation** — A user who wants "run prettier after every edit", "never let the agent `git push`" or "keep going until the tests pass" has to write a plugin.

**Approach** — Hook commands run as child processes exactly like the lifecycle hooks: argv spawn through `parseCommandToArgv` (no shell; a leading `~` is already expanded from HOME), the same `validateHookCommand` lint, process cwd = the session's `LoopHandlerContext.workdir`, else the repo root the config was loaded for. The hook receives one JSON object on stdin and answers by exit code plus optional JSON on stdout.

**Trust.** A project `.nax/hooks.json` is repository-controlled code. `nax chat` refuses an untrusted project at its entry (`ensureProjectTrusted` in `packages/nax/src/cli/chat.ts`), but `nax run` has no entry gate: each repository-controlled execution site asserts trust itself, e.g. `fireHook` calls `assertTrusted(workdir, "hooks")` before every project lifecycle hook (`packages/nax/src/hooks/runner.ts`). Agent hooks follow the same per-site rule: before spawning an entry with `origin: "project"`, `runAgentHook` calls `assertTrusted(cwd, "agent-hooks")` (new `TrustSurface` member `"agent-hooks"` in `packages/nax/src/trust/types.ts`); a refusal becomes a hook error and nothing is spawned, so a project `PreToolUse` hook in an untrusted project fails closed. Global entries are exempt, as in `fireHook`.

**Environment and output.** The hook process gets the same allowed environment the lifecycle hooks get (`buildAllowedEnv`, as `executeHook` does). Hook output is not size-capped (see Out of Scope).

Exit codes: `0` = success; `2` = block, reason = trimmed stderr; any other code, a timeout, a spawn failure or a lint rejection = hook error. Optional stdout JSON on exit 0 (a stdout that is not a JSON object is plain text). Empty block stderr falls back to a reason naming the command. Trim stdout and return the original parsed object only for non-null non-array objects. A whitespace-only command answers error reason `empty command`.

`validateHookCommand(command: string): void` (throws `NaxError` code `HOOK_DANGEROUS_PATTERN`, exported) and the spawn/kill pattern of `executeHook` (`Bun.spawn` with `detached: true`, SIGTERM then SIGKILL after a grace period, stream capture with a drain bound) — `packages/nax/src/hooks/runner.ts`. `killProcessGroup` is imported there from `@nathapp/nax-agent/internal`, and `run-hook.ts` imports it the same way; `captureStream`, `waitForDrain` and `HOOK_KILL_GRACE_MS` are module-private and `runner.ts` is out of scope, so `run-hook.ts` MIRRORS those with its own private helpers.

Every hook execution logs one `agent-hooks` debug line with the event, command, outcome kind and duration in ms. Read killGraceMs/drainMs at call time. A timeout reason contains `timed out after <timeoutMs>ms`; escalation must terminate TERM-resistant process groups. Drain stdout and stderr concurrently with a bound and retain partial output even when a descendant holds a pipe open. Catch trust/lint/spawn failures into AgentHookRun.error, never reject; cancel timers/readers on completion. Export the new runner symbols from the agent barrel.

**Scope** — In: run-hook.ts, TrustSurface addition and execution tests. Out: config parsing (US-002), event-specific interpretation (US-004/005), surface loading/wiring (US-006/007). No changes to runner.ts.
- Any change to the run lifecycle hooks (`on-*` events), `loadHooksConfig`, `executeHook` or `fireHook`.
- A size cap on hook stdout, stderr or context injected into the prompt or a tool result.
- Killing a running hook when the turn is aborted: `LoopHandlerContext` carries no abort signal by design, so a hook runs until it exits or reaches its own `timeout`.

**Interface** —
```typescript
// run-hook.ts
export const AGENT_HOOK_KILL_GRACE_MS = 5000;
export const AGENT_HOOK_DRAIN_MS = 2000;
export const AGENT_HOOK_HANDLER_MARGIN_MS = 1000;
export const _runAgentHookDeps: {
  killGraceMs: number; // default AGENT_HOOK_KILL_GRACE_MS, read at call time
  drainMs: number; // default AGENT_HOOK_DRAIN_MS, read at call time
  assertTrusted: typeof assertTrusted;
};
export function runAgentHook(input: {
  readonly entry: AgentHookEntry;
  readonly payload: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;
  readonly cwd: string;
}): Promise<AgentHookRun>;
```

**Goal** — Build the surface-scoped factory's tool handlers with sequential decisions, input rewrite chaining and session-isolated post-tool input records.

**Motivation** — A user who wants "run prettier after every edit", "never let the agent `git push`" or "keep going until the tests pass" has to write a plugin.

**Approach** — One factory per surface turns the `agent` section of `hooks.json` into a `LoopHandlerSet`. It registers at most one handler per loop event; that handler runs every applicable hook entry for the event in order. One handler per event (not per entry) is required: `dispatchChain` in `packages/nax-agent/src/native/session/loop-events/registry.ts` merges handler patches by overwrite, so two handlers that each return a `before_turn` patch would keep only the last one's.

`PreToolUse` maps to `before_tool`, before each tool call whose name the entry's `matcher` matches. `PostToolUse` maps to `after_tool`, after each tool call whose name the entry's `matcher` matches. Scope-filter once at factory construction; matcher absent or `*` matches all tools; otherwise use the anchored full-name expression `^(?:matcher)$`.

**Deadline.** Each factory handler's `timeoutMs` is `AGENT_HOOK_HANDLER_MARGIN_MS + Σ (entry.timeoutMs + AGENT_HOOK_KILL_GRACE_MS + AGENT_HOOK_DRAIN_MS)` over the entries that handler may run on its surface (for `before_turn`: every `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` entry, whether or not `SessionStart` runs on a given turn). The constants live in `run-hook.ts`: `AGENT_HOOK_KILL_GRACE_MS = 5000` (SIGTERM to SIGKILL), `AGENT_HOOK_DRAIN_MS = 2000` (stream drain bound), `AGENT_HOOK_HANDLER_MARGIN_MS = 1000`. With this bound a hook always reports its own timeout or error before the wrapper's deadline fires, so the per-event failure rules below, not the wrapper's generic ones, decide the outcome.

**Combining several entries of one event** (one handler runs them all, in config order):
- Context strings (`additionalContext`, and plain-text stdout where it counts as context) are joined with `"\n\n"` in entry order; empty strings are dropped.
- `PreToolUse`: an entry's `updatedInput` becomes the `tool_input` every later entry sees and the outcome's `input`; context strings become one `nudge` text; the first block stops the chain. An outcome with both an input and a nudge text is `{ kind: "nudge", text, input }`.
- `PostToolUse`: each entry's feedback (`"Hook feedback: " + reason` on a block) and context (`"Hook context: " + text`) is appended to the result content in entry order, each preceded by `"\n\n"`.
- A JSON-object stdout is never treated as plain text: a `SessionStart` entry answering `{}` adds no context.
- A JSON `decision: "block"` without a string `reason` uses the reason `blocked by hook '<command>'`.
- A `PreToolUse` `updatedInput` that is not a JSON object is ignored with one warning naming the command.

stdin JSON (every event): `hook_event_name`, `session_id` (the context's `sessionName`), `cwd`, `surface` (`"chat"` | `"run"`), and, when the context has them, `role`, `feature`, `story_id`, `model`. Per event:
- `PreToolUse`: `tool_name`, `tool_input` (the input as rewritten by earlier hooks in the same chain).
- `PostToolUse`: `tool_name`, `tool_input`, `tool_response` = `{ "content": string, "is_error": boolean }` (`is_error` is `false` when the payload's `isError` is absent). `tool_input` is the input the agent-hooks `before_tool` handler recorded for that call: the call's input after this handler's own `updatedInput` rewrites (a later plugin's rewrite is not seen). The record is keyed by the context's `sessionName` plus the call id, is written only when at least one `PostToolUse` entry matches the tool name, and is deleted on every `after_tool` dispatch for that key, whether or not a hook runs. `tool_input` is omitted when the payload has no `callId` or nothing was recorded. (A blocked call never reaches `after_tool`; its record stays until the factory is dropped at the end of the chat or run.)

Use ctx.workdir as cwd, falling back to repoRoot. Post-only config needs a recording before_tool entry with timeoutMs 1000. PreToolUse block returns `{ kind: "block", content: "Blocked by hook: " + reason }`; an error fails closed with isError true and content naming command/error. PostToolUse errors warn and contribute no patch, while block adds feedback rather than blocking the already-finished call. Read decision/reason/updatedInput/additionalContext only for PreToolUse and decision/reason/additionalContext for PostToolUse. Plain stdout is ignored for tool events. Call the runner through _agentHookHandlerDeps for every entry and add factory exports to the agent barrel.

**Scope** — In: factory, tool events, surface filters, timeout accounting, shared record and tool-handler tests. Out: turn-event handlers (US-005), loader/chat (US-006) and run setup (US-007). 
- Merging a hook's `PreToolUse` nudge with rule delivery's nudge for the same call; the later handler's nudge wins (rule delivery, on both surfaces).
- Removing the `tool_input` record of a call that a later handler blocked; it lives until the chat or run ends.

**Interface** —
```typescript
// handlers.ts
export const AGENT_HOOKS_PLUGIN = "agent-hooks";
export const _agentHookHandlerDeps: { runAgentHook: typeof runAgentHook };
export function createAgentHookHandlers(input: {
  readonly config: AgentHooksConfig;
  readonly surface: AgentHookSurface;
  readonly repoRoot: string;
}): LoopHandlerSet;
```
